  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>The beaches of Fréjus and Saint-Aygulf are closed to dogs, except on a few marked stretches. At Les Esclamandes, in Saint-Aygulf, two zones are open to them as long as they are on a leash: this is the "Saint-Aygulf beach for dogs" of the Fréjus tourist office (located around 43.402 / 6.731).</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>Article 10 of municipal order no. 2026-1797 (dated 4 June 2026, valid 4 June to 20 September 2026) bans dogs from the beaches and calanques except on three stretches marked by signs, dog on a leash: two at Les Esclamandes (west of the beach access channel K, and 110 m east of that channel K, where naturism is tolerated) and one at the Base Nature, between channel I and lifeguard post no. 6 "Pacha" (see <a href="/en/fiche-plage-pacha-frejus">Plage du Pacha</a>). Outside these zones, do not take your dog onto the sand. The order sets no hours for dogs, but it only covers the 2026 summer season: outside it, ask the tourist office. For lifeguards, the Argens post is staffed 9am-7pm from 6 June to 31 August, and the Esclamandes and Galiote posts are also open. No official source we read details showers, shade or water points for dogs: bring water, a sunshade and poop bags.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: spot the start and end signs before setting up, and keep your dog on a leash everywhere, including in the water.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>Plage du Pacha, Fréjus's other dog beach, is on the Base Nature: the Argens river mouth separates the two banks, so you cannot walk there from here. The Villepey ponds right behind are suitable for a walk on a leash; swimming is banned there according to Le Var Tourisme.</p>
